@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+## Context
+
+`json-pointer.ts` has a private `parsePointer`; patches also need parent lookup and the `-` array token, which `resolvePointer` rejects.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** strict RFC 6902 behaviour, no input mutation, atomic result (error means no result).
+**Non-Goals:** JSON Merge Patch, diff generation.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Export `parsePointer` from `json-pointer.ts` for reuse; pointer syntax errors are wrapped as `JsonPatchError`.
+- Deep-clone the document up front (`structuredClone`) and operate on the clone; values from the patch are cloned when inserted so the patch is not aliased or mutated. Atomicity follows because errors discard the clone.
+- Array indexes must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*`; `add` allows index == length and `-`; other ops require existing elements.
+- `test` uses a recursive deep equality: object key order ignored, numbers compared by value, types strict.
+- `move` from a location that is a proper prefix of `path` is an error; `from === path` is a no-op (after checking `from` exists). `copy` clones the value.
+- Whole-document ops (`""` path) replace the root; `remove` of root is an error (no valid document result is specified; assume error).
+- Validation: patch must be an array; each op an object with a valid `op` string, string `path`, `value` present (own property) for add/replace/test, string `from` for move/copy. Unknown ops throw.
+- Prototype safety: use own-property checks and define members via `Object.defineProperty`-safe assignment so `__proto__` keys are ordinary members.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Cloning cost is O(document) per call; acceptable for simplicity.
